/-!
# The global extended Čech complex of a globally generated ideal sheaf

[Stacks, Tag 099E] asks, for a finite-type sheaf of ideals `ℐ ⊆ 𝒪` on a ringed site `(𝒞, 𝒪)`,
for a map `K ⟶ 𝒪` in `D(𝒪)` such that

> for every `U ∈ Ob(𝒞)` such that `ℐ|_U` is generated by `f_1, …, f_r ∈ ℐ(U)` there is an
> isomorphism `(𝒪_U → ∏_{i₀} 𝒪_{U, f_{i₀}} → ⋯ → 𝒪_{U, f_1 ⋯ f_r}) ⟶ K|_U` compatible with
> maps to `𝒪_U`.

This file states that condition — `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.IsGlobalExtendedCech` — and
proves it, with no hypotheses, for `ℐ` generated by finitely many *global* sections, of the
extended alternating Čech complex of those sections with the augmentation `Č(f) ⟶ 𝒪` of
`Completion.lean`. The tag for an arbitrary sheaf of ideals is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_isGlobalExtendedCech` of `FiniteTypeExtendedCech.lean`.

The one input it needs — that the Čech complex in `D(𝒪_U)` does not depend on which finite
family generating `ℐ|_U` is used — is proved here as
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.cechIndependentOfRestriction_idealOfGlobalSections`, out of three
facts proved elsewhere: two finite families of global sections generating the same sheaf of ideals
give isomorphic objects of `D(𝒪)` over `𝒪` for the *sheafified presheaf of Čech complexes*
(`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_iso_sheafifiedCech_of_idealOfGlobalSections_eq` of
`SheafifiedCechLocalIndependence.lean`); that object is the extended alternating Čech complex
(`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.sheafifiedCechIsoExtendedAlternatingCech` of
`SheafifiedCechAlternating.lean`), compatibly with the two augmentations
(`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.sheafifiedCechIsoExtendedAlternatingCech_hom_comp_cechAugmentation`
of `SheafifiedCechAugmentation.lean`); and the restricted family generates the restricted ideal
sheaf (`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overIdeal_idealOfGlobalSections` of
`OverIdealGlobalSections.lean`).

**The generating family is quantified universally and the isomorphism asserted to exist for
each one.** The condition therefore involves no choice of generating family, and in particular
nothing functorial in the object: a functorial choice of Čech generators does not exist
(`Stacks.FunctorialCechFamily.not_transportedFamily` of
`StacksFrontier/RingTheory/Cech/FunctorialCechFamilyObstruction.lean`), and the condition is stated
and — in the globally generated case — proved here without one. What the source's *proof* needs
[Stacks, Tag 099D] for is its gluing: it builds `K` out of local models `K_{U, I}` on each
slice, as the sheafification of the presheaf of complexes `U'/U ↦ K(𝒪(U'), I𝒪(U'))`, which is a
presheaf only because `K(-, -)` is functorial in the ring. Where a single global complex is
available, as it is for a globally generated `ℐ`, there is nothing to glue and hence no
functorial datum to choose.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.unitIsoCechXZero`: the augmentation `𝒪 ⟶ Č(f)^0` into the
  degree-zero term, as an isomorphism, with `cechAugmentationZero` of `Completion.lean` for its
  inverse.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.restrictStructureSheafMap`: the restriction to a slice of a map
  of complexes to the structure sheaf in degree zero.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.IsGlobalExtendedCech`: **the condition of
  [Stacks, Tag 099E]** on a map `K ⟶ 𝒪` of complexes.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.CechIndependentOfRestriction`: the generator-independence
  statement the theorem below is proved from, itself proved below.

## Main results

* `HomologicalComplex.comp_mkHomToSingle` and `HomologicalComplex.map_mkHomToSingle`: a map into
  an object placed in a single degree is compatible with a map of complexes matching the two
  maps out of the degree-`j` terms, and with an additive functor.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_extendedAlternatingCechAugmentation`: the augmentation
  `𝒪 ⟶ Č(f)^0` is an isomorphism, the degree-zero term being the single factor
  `𝒪_{f_∅} = 𝒪_1`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.restrictStructureSheafMap_cechAugmentation`:
  **`Č(f)|_U ≅ Č(f|_U)` carries the restriction of `Č(f) ⟶ 𝒪` to `Č(f|_U) ⟶ 𝒪_U`.**
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.cechIndependentOfRestriction_idealOfGlobalSections`:
  **the Čech complex on a slice depends only on the ideal sheaf the family generates**, for
  `ℐ = (f_1, …, f_r)` generated by finitely many global sections.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_isGlobalExtendedCech_idealOfGlobalSections`:
  **the condition of [Stacks, Tag 099E] for `ℐ = (f_1, …, f_r)` generated by finitely many global
  sections, met by the Čech complex of the generators**, with no hypotheses.

## Implementation notes

The map `Č(f) ⟶ 𝒪` is not built here: it is `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.cechAugmentation` of
`Completion.lean`, where the map out of the Čech complex is what derived internal Hom turns into
the completion map `K ⟶ K^∧` of [Stacks, Tag 099B]. What is added is its compatibility with
restriction to a slice, and for that two general facts about
`HomologicalComplex.mkHomToSingle` — the constructor `cechAugmentation` is built with — are
proved first, in the generality of an arbitrary complex shape and degree.

The augmentation `𝒪 ⟶ Č(f)^0` of `ExtendedCech.lean` and the identification
`cechAugmentationZero : Č(f)^0 ⟶ 𝒪` of `Completion.lean` are mutually inverse, which is
`unitIsoCechXZero` here; neither file says so, each using only the direction it needs. The proof
is that the degree-zero term is a product over a `Unique` index type, so the projection at `∅`
is an isomorphism (`CategoryTheory.Limits.productUniqueIso`), and that `𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪_{f_∅}` is an
isomorphism because `f_∅ = 1` is a unit (`unitIsoAwayModuleEmpty`).

The compatibility of the restriction to a slice with the maps to `𝒪` is then the augmentation
compatibility `overFunctor_map_extendedAlternatingCechAugmentation_comp` of
`CechSliceRestriction.lean` read through the inverse of the augmentation, which is the iso
calculus `Iso.eq_inv_comp` and `Iso.comp_inv_eq` and no more.

*Why the proofs here are in term mode.* `(AlternatingCech.complex F).X 0`, the degree-zero term
of the complex, and `AlternatingCech.term F 0`, the product it is defined as, are the same
object only after an unexposed definition is unfolded, which is invisible at the transparency
`rw` checks its result at; so is the identification of `X.ringSheaf.over U` with
`(X.over U).ringSheaf` on which every statement crossing between restriction to a slice and the
slice ringed site rests (see the "Why the two proofs here go through abstract lemmas" note of
`CechSliceRestriction.lean`). Every statement here is therefore either confined to a single
category with a single spelling of its objects — which is what the `HomologicalComplex` section
is for — or proved by `exact`, where the elaborator does unfold those definitions.

### What remains for Tag 099E

Relative to the four points of the "What remains for Tag 099E" section of
`CechGlobalSliceAway.lean`:

1. `Č(f)|_U ≅ Č(f|_U)` over `𝒪_U`. Proved there for the augmentations `𝒪 ⟶ Č^0`; extended here
   to the maps `Č ⟶ 𝒪` that the tag's compatibility clause is about.
2. **Independence of the chosen finite generating family, at the level of sheaves.** **Closed.**
   It is `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.cechIndependentOfRestriction_idealOfGlobalSections` below,
   and the route to it is not the one guessed here: it does not go through [Stacks, Tag 0997] or
   the short exact sequence of [Stacks, Tag 00EJ] at all. The comparison maps of the *presheaves*
   of Čech complexes exist with no spanning hypothesis, and after sheafification being a
   quasi-isomorphism is a local condition, so the covering on which two generating families span
   the same ideal of sections suffices — that is
   `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_iso_sheafifiedCech_of_idealOfGlobalSections_eq`. What
   remained was to read that statement, which is about the sheafified presheaf of Čech complexes,
   in the spelling the tag displays, which is the two identifications of
   `SheafifiedCechAlternating.lean` and `SheafifiedCechAugmentation.lean`.
3. A strictly functorial model, i.e. [Stacks, Tag 099D]: **not needed** for a globally generated
   `ℐ`, as the theorem below shows, and not needed for the *statement* in any case.
4. Gluing and descent: likewise **not needed** for a globally generated `ℐ`, and not needed for
   a general `ℐ` either: `FiniteTypeExtendedCech.lean` proves the tag for an arbitrary sheaf of
   ideals with an object that is a presheaf of complexes on the whole site before
   sheafification — the colimit over all local generators — so there is nothing to glue and no
   reduction to the subcategory of [Stacks, Tags 03CG, 03A0].

One further gap used to be visible in the hypothesis: nothing here proved that the restricted
family `f|_U` generates `ℐ|_U`, i.e. that
`idealOfGlobalSections (X.over U) (f|_U) = overIdeal (idealOfGlobalSections X f) U`. **It is
now proved**, as `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overIdeal_idealOfGlobalSections` of
`OverIdealGlobalSections.lean`: the containment `≤` is immediate from
`idealOfGlobalSections_le`, and the reverse is the sheafification description
`mem_idealOfGlobalSections_iff` together with the comparison of the covering sieves of `J` and
of `J.over U` — `CategoryTheory.Sieve.overEquiv` and
`CategoryTheory.GrothendieckTopology.mem_over_iff` — and nothing else. The hypothesis of the
theorem below is still stated relative to `f|_U` rather than to `ℐ|_U`, because that is the
family its conclusion is about; what the equality adds is that the restricted family does
satisfy the generation condition quantified over in `IsGlobalExtendedCech`, so the universally
quantified `g` there really does include `f|_U`.

The same comparison of sieves, applied to a family of sections of `ℐ` over `U` rather than to
the restrictions of global sections, is what lets [Stacks, Tag 099B] reach a *finite-type* `ℐ`
on each slice of a cover: see
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_coversTop_isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_overIdeal` of
`FiniteTypeLocalReflector.lean`, which is the local half of [Stacks, Tag 099F] at the tag's own
hypothesis. It does not supply item 2 above, which is a statement about Čech complexes and not
about ideal sheaves.

## References

